<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramanathaswamy Temple</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ramanathaswamy Temple is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rameswaram">Rameswaram</a> island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, one of the twelve jyotirlingas and a major pilgrimage site linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>'s return from Lanka. Its present structure was largely built from the 12th century onward, and the Sethupathi rulers of Ramnad added its famous pillared corridors in the 17th and 18th centuries, among the longest of any Hindu temple.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramanathaswamy-temple.jpg" alt="Ramanathaswamy Temple" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ssriram mt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramanathaswamy_temple7.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the Yuddha Kanda of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> tells <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a> on the way back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> of the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> as a lingam on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rameswaram">Rameswaram</a> before he built his bridge to Lanka, and calls the spot able to expiate great sins. In the Shiva Purana, Shiva appears to Rama there, grants him victory over Ravana and agrees to remain on the island. Believers hold that two lingams stand in the sanctum: the Ramalingam, made by Rama from sand, and the Vishvalingam, brought by Hanuman from Kailash, which by Rama's instruction is worshipped first. The temple is also one of the 276 Paadal Petra Sthalams praised in the songs of the Nayanar poet-saints <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appar">Appar</a>, Sundarar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambandar">Sambandar</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The temple was expanded in the 12th century under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya dynasty</a>, and the Sri Lankan kings Parakrama Bahu (1153–1186) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nissanka-malla">Nissanka Malla</a> contributed to its sanctum and its building. King Jeyaveera Cinkaiariyan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> (1380–1410) shipped stone blocks from the Koneswaram temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trincomalee">Trincomalee</a> to renovate the sanctum. According to the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Firishta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a>, general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rameswaram">Rameswaram</a> in the early 14th century, and the Sultanate's court historians record that he raided its temples. In the 17th century Dalavai Setupati built part of the main eastern gopuram, and between 1745 and 1837 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> gave rest houses to the temple.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A high wall encloses the precinct, with towers to the east and west; the main gopuram is 53 m tall. The third corridor, called the Chokkatan Mandapam, was built by Muthuramalinga Setupati, who ruled from 1763 to 1795, and his statue and those of his two ministers stand at its western entrance. The corridors measure about 3,850 feet in all, and the outer corridor has about 1,212 pillars, most carved with individual compositions. Twenty-two holy water bodies lie within the temple, said to stand for the arrows of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>'s quiver, and bathing in them is central to the pilgrimage. The temple is one of the Char Dham sites and the southernmost of the twelve jyotirlingas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramanathaswamy Temple” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They endowed the <b>Ramanathaswamy</b> temple at Rameswaram, building its long corridors.</blockquote><a href="/state/ramnad-kingdom">Ramnad Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
